{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788190564,"nonce":"Ox4aauGV3c4lLRHPNblLEoRHEq4=","message":"DESIGN INVESTIGATION RESULT — two corrections and one addition to this issue's\nplan. Full discussion with Evan pending; recording the source-confirmed parts now\nso they are not lost.\n\n**Correction 1 — a better hook than the one deferred here.** This issue's note\nnamed `Pawn_JobTracker.EndCurrentJob` and correctly said it misses the `StopAll`\npaths (death, despawn, drafting, mental break). Both funnel through\n`CleanupCurrentJob`, which calls `curDriver.Cleanup(condition)` — and\n`Verse.AI/JobDriver.cs` `public void Cleanup(JobCondition)` is PUBLIC, with\n`public Pawn pawn` and `public Job job` on the same object. A read-only postfix\nthere gets `(pawn, job, condition)` for free AND catches the paths\n`EndCurrentJob` misses. It also fits `JournalHooks.cs`'s stated profile.\n\n**Correction 2 — `playerForced` alone is not the discriminator**, and this issue\nimplies it is. `RimWorld/JobGiver_Work.cs` `TryIssueJobPackage` sets\n`playerForced = true` autonomously on its emergency-prioritized branch. The\nunambiguous signature is the TRIPLE: `jobGiver is ThinkNode_QueuedJob \u0026\u0026\nworkGiverDef != null \u0026\u0026 playerForced`. Caveat: `Job.jobGiver` is scribed via\n`UniqueSaveKey`, so any mod that adds or removes a think node shifts the keys\nand yields a null `jobGiver` on existing saves. On a 38-mod bench, best-effort.\n\n**Addition — the fix this issue is missing, and it is the most important one for\nthe COST goal.** `Outcome.Result` does `Accepted.Count \u003e 0 ? Stamp(seq) :\nNoStamp()`, and every caller does `outcome.Count \u003e 0 ? Act(…) : 0`. So a fully\nredundant call writes NO `action` journal row at all — **the wasted orders are\nexactly the ones invisible to the ledger.** Since the journal IS the aggregate\nthe agent would learn from (`journal {types:[\"action\"]}` replays every order),\nan order that did nothing must still write a row, carrying its verdict\n(`accepted` / `already-doing-it` / `already-designated` / `rejected`). Without\nthat, \"which of my instructions are redundant\" is unanswerable at session end no\nmatter how good the per-call reporting is.\n\n**Cheap win found alongside, worth folding in here:** `Designator_Mine\n.CanDesignateCell` returns a bare rejected `AcceptanceReport` for an\nalready-designated cell and `Designator_Hunt.CanDesignateThing` returns bare\n`false`, so `DesignateEngine.ReasonOf` — correctly refusing to invent a reason —\nemits `{why:\"not-designatable\", reason:null}`, indistinguishable from \"not\nmineable\". A `DesignationManager.DesignationOn/At` pre-check emitting\n`already-designated` as its own reject key is ~5 lines and makes\n`rejects_by_reason.already-designated` a free per-call count of wasted orders.\n\n**Confirmed limit, to keep this issue honest:** none of the above answers \"would\nthey have done it anyway\". `already-doing-it` catches the collision only at the\ninstant of the call — a pawn three ticks from choosing the same job on its own\nstill reads as a clean `accepted`. The only rigorous answer is a hold-out (stop\nsending a class of instruction for N days and compare), which is a playbook\nlesson, not a feature.","files":null}]}